WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.120
Embedded devices may store device
identities, certificates, tokens,

2
00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:07.518
API keys, encryption keys, signing keys,
provisioning records,

3
00:00:07.598 --> 00:00:08.865
or service credentials.

4
00:00:08.945 --> 00:00:12.927
These values affect how the device proves
what it is, which services it can use,

5
00:00:13.007 --> 00:00:14.696
and what operations are allowed.

6
00:00:14.776 --> 00:00:17.853
They need lifecycle ownership, not just a
storage location.

7
00:00:17.933 --> 00:00:22.035
Secrets should not be hardcoded in source
code, headers, sample configuration,

8
00:00:22.115 --> 00:00:25.011
build scripts, firmware images, logs, or
test fixtures.

9
00:00:25.091 --> 00:00:29.133
Hardcoded values are difficult to rotate,
easy to copy into multiple products,

10
00:00:29.213 --> 00:00:31.505
and hard to remove from development
history.

11
00:00:31.585 --> 00:00:35.869
Production identity should come from a
reviewed provisioning and storage process.

12
00:00:35.949 --> 00:00:39.629
Provisioning should define how secrets are
created, injected, stored,

13
00:00:39.709 --> 00:00:41.338
rotated, revoked, and retired.

14
00:00:41.418 --> 00:00:44.856
It should also define who owns each step,
what evidence is recorded,

15
00:00:44.936 --> 00:00:46.927
what happens on provisioning failure,

16
00:00:47.007 --> 00:00:50.024
and how replacement devices or repaired
units are handled.

17
00:00:50.104 --> 00:00:54.206
Manufacturing convenience should not
become field-device policy by accident.

18
00:00:54.286 --> 00:00:58.207
Hardware-backed storage, secure elements,
TPMs, protected flash regions,

19
00:00:58.287 --> 00:01:02.269
or platform key storage may be appropriate
depending on device capability and

20
00:01:02.349 --> 00:01:03.073
product risk.

21
00:01:03.153 --> 00:01:06.351
The course does not prescribe one
mechanism for every product.

22
00:01:06.431 --> 00:01:10.231
The key habit is to use documented
platform capabilities and write down the

23
00:01:10.311 --> 00:01:11.035
assumptions.

24
00:01:11.115 --> 00:01:14.494
Secrets in memory should have limited
lifetime, minimal copying,

25
00:01:14.574 --> 00:01:16.263
and careful logging discipline.

26
00:01:16.343 --> 00:01:18.877
Avoid formatting sensitive values into
strings,

27
00:01:18.957 --> 00:01:22.637
storing them in broad-scope buffers, or
including them in diagnostics.

28
00:01:22.717 --> 00:01:27.121
Clear sensitive buffers where the platform
and compiler support a reliable approach,

29
00:01:27.201 --> 00:01:31.002
while remembering that clearing is one
control, not a complete guarantee.

30
00:01:31.082 --> 00:01:35.184
Device identity should be scoped,
auditable, and tied to the product lifecycle.

31
00:01:35.264 --> 00:01:38.642
A manufacturing token, service credential,
device certificate,

32
00:01:38.722 --> 00:01:42.282
signing trust anchor, and local pairing
key have different purposes.

33
00:01:42.362 --> 00:01:46.163
Reviewers should be able to tell where
each one is used, what it authorizes,

34
00:01:46.243 --> 00:01:47.932
and how it is replaced or retired.

35
00:01:48.012 --> 00:01:50.063
Field support depends on this planning.

36
00:01:50.143 --> 00:01:53.280
When credentials expire, are rejected, or
need replacement,

37
00:01:53.360 --> 00:01:57.282
the device should report bounded status
without exposing sensitive values.

38
00:01:57.362 --> 00:02:00.740
Good identity design makes both security
and maintenance easier.
